menu "My UPS Pal board"

choice UPSA_BOARD
    prompt "Target board"
    default UPSA_BOARD_REV_A
    help
        Selects the pin map and peripheral set.

        Rev A is the board that exists and ships. Rev A OLED is the same PCB,
        built as a separate identity for a module labelled VCC, GND, SCL, SDA
        seated in J3; see docs/hardware.md, "OLED module compatibility". Rev B
        (docs/rev-b.md) has the same pin map on an N16R8 module -- 16 MB
        flash, 8 MB octal PSRAM -- and an OLED header in the standard module
        order. Its build must also load sdkconfig.defaults.revb, which sets
        the flash size, the partition table and PSRAM; see that file.

        The two dev board choices are for people who flash a generic
        ESP32-S3 board themselves (docs/dev-board.md). They are build from
        source only: never a release, never offered an update, and each
        carries its own image name so it can never take a Rev A or Rev B
        image, or the other way round.

        Application code must never reference a GPIO number directly. Use
        the BOARD_* macros from board.h.

config UPSA_BOARD_REV_A
    bool "My UPS Pal, Rev A PCB"

config UPSA_BOARD_REVA_OLED
    bool "My UPS Pal, Rev A PCB, with a VCC-first OLED module fitted"
    select UPSA_REVA_OLED_VCC_FIRST
    help
        Same PCB as the plain Rev A choice above. A separate board IDENTITY,
        not a separate board: that is what makes it safe to give this build
        to someone who is not sitting at a bench. A plain Rev A board can
        never be offered this image, and a board already running it can
        never be silently offered a plain Rev A release that would leave its
        screen dark, because the two carry different BOARD_OTA_TARGET /
        BOARD_IMAGE_NAME identities and the OTA client refuses a mismatch.

        ONLY for a display module whose pins are labelled VCC, GND, SCL,
        SDA, in that order. Rev A's J3 reads 3V3, GND, SDA, SCL from pin 1:
        a standard GND-first module seated in it is powered backwards and
        dies, and no firmware setting can prevent that -- read the labels on
        the module in hand before plugging it in, with VCC into J3's square
        pad. Selecting this pulls in UPSA_REVA_OLED_VCC_FIRST and the display
        automatically; neither needs setting separately.

config UPSA_BOARD_REV_B
    bool "My UPS Pal, Rev B PCB (N16R8)"

config UPSA_BOARD_DEV_S3
    bool "ESP32-S3 dev board, no PSRAM (build from source)"
    help
        Any ESP32-S3 board with GPIO19/20 wired to a USB-A port that supplies
        5 V to the UPS. No load switch, no status LEDs, no display: the
        features of Rev A, with recovery by re-enumeration only. Uses the
        4 MB partition table, so it runs on 4, 8 and 16 MB parts, with or
        without PSRAM (PSRAM is left unused).

config UPSA_BOARD_DEV_S3_PSRAM
    bool "ESP32-S3 dev board, N16R8 (build from source)"
    help
        The same, on an N16R8 module: Rev B's own module, 16 MB flash and
        8 MB octal PSRAM. Gets Rev B's features: Tailscale, HTTPS, the outage
        log and long history. Build it with sdkconfig.defaults.revb then
        sdkconfig.defaults.dev-s3-psram layered on top, so its flash layout
        and memory settings are Rev B's and follow them; see that file. An
        N8R8 or N8R2 takes the no-PSRAM choice above.

endchoice

# Boards with Rev B's module (N16R8: 8 MB octal PSRAM, 16 MB flash): what
# Tailscale, HTTPS and the outage log need. Not user-visible. Rev B, and the
# PSRAM dev board that takes Rev B's flash layout and memory settings.
config UPSA_BOARD_PSRAM_CLASS
    bool
    default y if UPSA_BOARD_REV_B
    default y if UPSA_BOARD_DEV_S3_PSRAM

# The network is wired Ethernet instead of Wi-Fi. Not user-visible: a fact about
# the board. Boards without a radio cannot offer a setup access point either.
config UPSA_NET_ETHERNET
    bool

# Which boards can carry a display at all. Not user-visible: it is a fact about
# the board, and UPSA_HAVE_OLED is hidden entirely where it is false.
#
# NOT plain Rev A. Its OLED header reads 3V3, GND, SDA, SCL from pin 1; the
# modules people buy read GND, VCC, SCL, SDA, so one seated in it is powered
# backwards. That happened to the first Rev A board on 2026-09-25: the board
# got hot and the module died. Offering a display build for plain Rev A
# invites a repeat -- UPSA_BOARD_REVA_OLED above is the one Rev A choice that
# carries it, because that choice means the VCC-first module is fitted. Rev
# B's J3 reads GND, 3V3, SCL, SDA, the modules' own order, so it needs none
# of this.
config UPSA_BOARD_OLED_OK
    bool
    default y if UPSA_BOARD_REV_B
    default y if UPSA_BOARD_REVA_OLED

# Internal, and not independently settable: pulled in by UPSA_BOARD_REVA_OLED
# above, never set on its own. It used to be a standalone "bench only" toggle
# that could sit on top of plain Rev A, which meant a build could carry the
# VCC-first pin swap without any OTA identity saying so -- exactly the gap
# that made the swap unsafe to hand to anyone off the bench. Tying it to the
# board choice instead means the pin swap and the identity that advertises it
# can never drift apart.
config UPSA_REVA_OLED_VCC_FIRST
    bool
    help
        Swaps SDA and SCL (GPIO9/GPIO8) for the display module, to match a
        module whose own pins read VCC, GND, SCL, SDA rather than the
        standard GND-first order. Set only by UPSA_BOARD_REVA_OLED.

config UPSA_HAVE_OLED
    bool "Enable optional I2C OLED status display"
    depends on UPSA_BOARD_OLED_OK
    default y if UPSA_BOARD_REVA_OLED
    default n
    help
        The web interface is the primary UI. The OLED is a debug convenience
        and is the single most expensive item on the BOM, so it is off by
        default, and firmware does not require it.

        Only offered on boards whose header takes a standard module as
        seated. Plain Rev A's does not; see UPSA_BOARD_OLED_OK.

config UPSA_MEM_DIAG
    bool "Log heap and task stack use every 10 s (diagnostics)"
    default n
    select FREERTOS_USE_TRACE_FACILITY
    help
        Logs internal heap free, lowest-ever free and largest free block, and
        every task's unused stack. For sizing stacks and buffers against
        measured use rather than guesses; off in releases, where the trace
        facility it needs costs a little RAM of its own.

config UPSA_CRASH_TEST
    bool "Bench only: cause an interrupt-watchdog crash a minute after boot"
    default n
    help
        Tests the crash recorder (components/crashlog): one minute after boot
        a task turns interrupts off and spins until the interrupt watchdog
        resets the board, which is what boards in the field have been seen to
        do. The next boot shows where on the page. Never in a release.

config UPSA_VBUS_CYCLE_MS
    int "VBUS off-time when power-cycling a wedged UPS interface (ms)"
    default 2000
    range 250 10000

endmenu
